+1.1 In this notice "the Act" means the National Credit Act 34 of 2005, "you" means TEST Borrower and "we" means TEST
+Credit Provider, registered credit provider NCRCP00000. On 13 October 2026 you gave notice under section 127(1) of the Act
+to terminate agreement AF-NCA-S127-TEST and surrender the asset described above. This is our notice under section
+127(2) of the Act: the estimated value of the asset is R 1,250.00, as valued by TEST Valuer on 20 October 2026. The estimate
+is not a guaranteed sale price.
+1.2 Within 10 business days after you receive this notice you may unconditionally withdraw your termination notice and
+resume possession of the asset, unless you are in default under the agreement, as section 127(3) of the Act provides. To
+withdraw, tell us in writing at the address in the letterhead.
+1.3 If you do not withdraw within that period, we must sell the asset as soon as practicable for the best price reasonably
+obtainable, as section 127(4) of the Act requires. We will not sell it before that period ends. Until the sale, the agreement
+continues and the amounts due under it remain payable.
+1.4 After the sale we will credit your account with the proceeds less the reasonable costs of the sale, and send you a written
+notice of the settlement value of the agreement immediately before the sale, the gross amount realised, the net proceeds and
+the amount credited or debited to your account, as section 127(5) of the Act requires. If the net proceeds exceed the
+settlement value we will pay you the difference. If they are less, we will demand the balance, and you will have 10 business
+days after receiving that demand to pay it before we may begin legal proceedings for it.
+1.5 If you dispute this valuation, tell us in writing within the 10 business days in clause 1.2. After the sale, section 128 of the
+Act allows you to apply to the National Consumer Tribunal to review it.
